  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 1237 AM MST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nTemperatures will run cooler on Sunday in the wake of Saturday's \nshortwaves and frontal passage. Readings in the eastern plains will \nobserve the biggest deficits of 5 to 10 degrees below normal while \nwestern and central areas remain near to slightly above. Winds aloft \nwill retain a northwesterly component, but will be much lighter in \nspeed while the surface high building into TX causes surface winds \nto veer southerly or westerly in NM. \n\nThe flow turns more zonal Sunday night into Monday with stiff speeds \n(15-40 kt at 700 mb). The previously advertised shortwave trough is \nlooking to be dampened a bit more with this past evening's model \nruns. This shortwave has always been resolved to be a dry feature, \nbut even recent runs bring up the speeds aloft slightly. The lee-\nside surface low will redevelop, but will not be all that deep \n(~1004 mb in northeast NM). This advertised regime should keep \nsurface speeds breezy to windy, but notably less than what is \nforecast for Saturday. The downsloping westerly winds in the plains \nwould allow temperatures to quickly rebound, putting all zones above \nnormal for Monday. \n\nThe flow aloft turns more southwesterly into Tuesday, decreasing in \nspeed a bit while the lee-side surface low redevelops in a weaker \nand less organized state. Dry and warmer than normal conditions \nwould persist with breezy to locally windy southwesterlies at the \nsurface.\n\nAttention will then focus due westward and upstream where a Pacific \nlow is still modeled to move through, arriving into NM on Wednesday \nand Thursday. While this system does not look to have any tap of \nsubtropical moisture, its maritime origins and sufficient \nbaroclinicity and dynamics should yield measurable precipitation for \nmuch of western and central NM, and overall this system's appearance \naligns better with the El Nino conceptual model for early December. \nPrecipitation would be harder to come by in the eastern plains \nWednesday and Thursday due to downsloping and a filling in of the \nupper low. All areas would likely drop back to, or slightly below \nnormal by Thursday.",
